---
title: "The property tax dividend from Edmonton's RS Zone rowhomes"
date: "2026-07-09"
author: "Jacob Dawang"
categories: [housing, edmonton, zoning]
description: "Redeveloping an old house into a 5-8 unit RS Zone rowhome doesn't just add homes, it multiplies the property's assessed value. Here's what that's worth to Edmonton's property tax base since the Zoning Bylaw Renewal took effect."
link-external-newwindow: true
link-external-icon: false
execute:
  cache: false
---

Edmonton's Zoning Bylaw Renewal (ZBR) has spent two years quietly rewriting mature neighbourhoods, one lot at a time. [Prior posts on this blog](../zbr-two-year-review/index.qmd) have tracked the homes it unlocked and the neighbourhoods it reached. This post asks a different question: what is that redevelopment worth to the City's property tax base?

When a single-family lot in a mature neighbourhood becomes a 5-8 unit RS Zone rowhome, its assessed value doesn't just grow, it multiplies. That's a durable increase in the tax base, not a one-time windfall: every year, that property contributes meaningfully more property tax than the house it replaced would have. Using building permits from 2024, the first year the RS Zone was in force, matched against the City's property assessment data, I measure that uplift directly for completed projects, then use a regression model fit on those measurements to estimate what it adds up to across the full 2024-2026 pipeline.

## Data

This analysis combines three of Edmonton's Open Data sources. Edmonton's [general building permit data](https://data.edmonton.ca/Urban-Planning-Economy/General-Building-Permits/24uj-dj8v/about_data), the same source used in the [Q2 2026 building permit report](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), identifies which properties were redeveloped into 5-8 unit RS Zone rowhomes and when. Edmonton's [current calendar year property assessment data](https://data.edmonton.ca/City-Administration/Property-Assessment-Data-Current-Calendar-Year-/q7d6-ambg/about_data) gives each property's assessed value as of 2026, and Edmonton's [historical property assessment data](https://data.edmonton.ca/City-Administration/Property-Assessment-Data-Historical-/qi6a-xuwt/about_data) gives assessed values back to 2012, including 2024 and 2025.

The key challenge is linking a specific building permit to the same physical property, before and after redevelopment. Account numbers usually persist through redevelopment, but addresses often don't: a lot can get re-addressed when it's rebuilt, so a permit's civic address is frequently *not* the address that appears in the property's earlier assessment record. Instead, I match on legal description (plan/block/lot), which is stable across redevelopment, with a fallback to geographic proximity for the small number of properties where the legal description format doesn't parse identically on both sides, or where redevelopment came with a brand new account number.




::: {.cell}

:::


## Measuring the uplift: the 2024 cohort

The 2026 property assessment is supposed to reflect each property's physical condition as of December 31, 2025. In principle, that means any 2024 permit whose building had received occupancy by the end of 2025 should show up, completed, in the 2026 assessment roll, giving a clean before-and-after pair. In practice, as we'll see below, the assessments only reliably catch up to buildings finished by about September 2025, so the measurement cohort uses that earlier cutoff.


::: {.cell}

:::


Edmonton issued **118** mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit new-build permits in 2024, of which **79** had received occupancy by the end of 2025. I match all 118 to their assessment records, then use the occupancy dates to decide which ones the 2026 assessment actually reflects as complete.

### Matching permits to assessments

I parse each side's legal description into a `(plan, block, lot)` key and join on it. A building permit's legal description looks like `Plan 715HW Blk 25 Lot 22`; the historical assessment data records the same parcel as `Plan: 715HW  Block: 25  Lot: 22`.


::: {.cell}

:::


That legal-key match alone links **110** of 118 properties (93%) to their 2024 baseline assessed value. For the remainder, I fall back to matching each permit to the nearest 2024 assessment point within 25m. I checked these fallback matches by hand: most are lots that were consolidated from two legal titles into one (a permit reads `Lots 2-3`, while the assessment record spells that out as two separate `Lot:` entries joined by " / ", which my parser doesn't follow), and in each case checked the fallback match has the exact same civic address as the permit, at a matched distance of a few millimetres.


::: {.cell}

:::


The 25m fallback brings the total match rate to **115** of 118 (97%). The remaining 3 properties have no corresponding parcel in the 2024 assessment roll at all, likely lots created by subdivision after that year's assessment date, so they're dropped from the analysis.

Each matched property's `account_number` persists through redevelopment in most cases, so I use it to look up the completed building's 2026 assessed value. A small number of properties are issued a brand new account number when redeveloped rather than keeping the old one, exactly the re-addressing situation described above; I confirmed one such case by hand, where the 25m fallback correctly landed on a new 2026 account whose civic address matched the permit's own address exactly, even though the old account (still on the 2024/2025 rolls under the pre-redevelopment address) had disappeared from the 2026 data entirely.


::: {.cell}

:::


That yields **115** 2024 permits with both a 2024 baseline and a 2026 assessed value.

### When does the assessment catch up?

Before treating every occupancy-granted building as "complete" in the 2026 data, it's worth checking whether the assessments actually keep up with late-year completions. @fig-assessment-lag plots each matched property's gross uplift against its occupancy date. The pattern is stark: buildings that received occupancy by roughly the end of September 2025 show the full rowhome uplift, while October, November, and especially December completions drop off a cliff — their 2026 assessed values still largely reflect a construction site, not a finished building, even though the assessment is nominally "as of December 31, 2025".


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Gross assessed value uplift by occupancy date: the 2026 assessment only reliably reflects buildings finished by about September 2025](index_files/figure-html/fig-assessment-lag-1.png){#fig-assessment-lag width=672}
:::
:::


So the measurement cohort for this post is the matched 2024 permits with **occupancy granted by September 30, 2025** — buildings whose 2026 assessment genuinely reflects the completed rowhome. Everything else, including those October-December completions, goes into the "not yet fully reflected" group.


::: {.cell}

:::


The measurement cohort is **48** properties. @tbl-spotlight traces two properties through the process, from their pre-redevelopment assessed value, through a mid-construction 2025 assessment, to their completed 2026 value. The Alberta Avenue example received occupancy in November 2025, yet its 2026 value looks fully assessed — a reminder that the lag in @fig-assessment-lag is a strong tendency, not a hard rule, which is why the conservative September cutoff is used for measurement.


::: {#tbl-spotlight .cell tbl-cap='Two properties traced from pre-redevelopment (2024) through mid-construction (2025) to a completed rowhome (2026)'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

