That gives a fully-observed total of **$128.71M** in assessed value uplift already showing up in the 2026 roll from the 2024 cohort alone, no assumptions needed. It understates the eventual value of that cohort, though: the "not yet fully reflected" group's uplift is a snapshot mid-construction (or a stale assessment of a just-finished building), not the finished-building value those properties will eventually reach. That gap between actual-so-far and eventual-once-complete is exactly what the model-based extrapolation later in this post is estimating.

### From gross uplift to a fair counterfactual

The raw 2024-to-2026 difference in assessed value overstates the effect of redevelopment on its own, because Edmonton property values were also generally rising over that period. To isolate the redevelopment effect, I build a counterfactual: what would each property likely have been worth in 2026 if it *hadn't* been redeveloped? I estimate this from a control group of RS-zoned properties in mature neighbourhoods that were not part of any 2024-2026 residential new-build permit, taking the median ratio of their 2026 to 2024 assessed value as a general appreciation factor.


::: {.cell}

:::


Across **79,874** non-redeveloped RS Zone properties in mature neighbourhoods, the median 2024-to-2026 growth ratio is **1.19x**, meaning a typical mature-neighbourhood house that wasn't touched still gained about 19% in assessed value over those two years. I use this as the baseline against which redevelopment uplift is measured: **net uplift** is the completed rowhome's 2026 value minus 1.19 times its 2024 baseline, isolating the redevelopment effect from general market appreciation. **Gross uplift** is the simple, unadjusted difference, shown alongside it for comparison.

@fig-uplift-distribution shows the distribution of both measures across the 48 matched properties, split by project size.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Distribution of assessed value uplift per redeveloped property, gross and net of market appreciation](index_files/figure-html/fig-uplift-distribution-1.png){#fig-uplift-distribution width=672}
:::
:::


@tbl-uplift-rules compares three simple ways of summarizing this uplift into a single per-project figure: a median per property within two size buckets (5-6 units and 7-8 units), a single pooled median per property across all sizes, and a median per home added, which can be scaled by any project's unit count. These serve as sanity anchors for the model-based rule developed next.


::: {#tbl-uplift-rules .cell tbl-cap='Three ways to summarize per-project uplift, gross and net of appreciation'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

