Units added is the clearest and most statistically solid effect in the model, but because it enters on a log scale, its dollar effect isn't constant per home — it tapers off as project size grows. Near the bottom of the 5-8 unit range, the marginal home is worth about **$342.30k**; near the top, about $213.94k, a diminishing-returns pattern consistent with each additional unit adding somewhat less land-value-per-unit than the last. A neighbourhood with double the typical RS-zoned land value tends to see about $334.85k more in uplift, holding project size and distance fixed, though the estimate is imprecise. Distance from LRT comes out slightly *positive*, around $116.11k per km, and statistically indistinguishable from zero: within mature neighbourhoods, being near a station doesn't measurably raise how much value a rowhome redevelopment adds, perhaps because station-adjacent land already carried a higher baseline value. With 48 observations, all of these intervals are wide, and the model explains a modest 28% of the variation, which is the honest state of knowledge one construction cycle into ZBR.

@fig-model-diagnostics shows the model's residual diagnostics: no strong funnel shape in the residuals-vs-fitted panel (the usual argument for a log transform) and reasonably normal-looking residuals.


