## From one cohort to the whole pipeline

The measured cohort only covers 2024 permits that were both matchable and fully reflected in the 2026 assessment. To estimate the cumulative effect of the RS Zone rowhome since ZBR took effect, I apply the uplift model from @tbl-uplift-model to every other mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit new-build permit issued since 2024: the rest of the 2024 cohort (permits that didn't match or aren't fully assessed yet), all of 2025, and 2026. Each permit gets a prediction from its own unit count, LRT distance, and neighbourhood.

This is a **steady-state estimate**: it assumes every one of those permits eventually reaches the completed-building uplift the model predicts, once construction wraps up and a future assessment catches up to it. It is not the smaller amount those properties currently show in the 2026 roll, which @tbl-2024-actual-total showed directly for 2024's not-yet-reflected permits. The gap between that actual, partial total and the steady-state estimate below is construction and assessment lag, not double-counting.


::: {.cell}

:::


2025 permitted **302** more mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit rowhomes, and H1 2026 has already permitted 190. Following the same [H1 annualization method used in the Q2 2026 report](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), full-year 2026 is projected at 2.1x the H1 count.

Predicting *net* uplift needs each permit's own 2024 baseline value, so the 2025 and 2026 permits are run through the same baseline matchers as the 2024 cohort (their lots all existed in the 2024 roll, pre-redevelopment).


::: {.cell}

:::


The 2025 and 2026 permits match their 2024 baselines at a **96%** rate; the few that don't use their neighbourhood's median RS value as a stand-in baseline for the net adjustment.

@fig-cumulative-value-uplift shows the resulting cumulative assessed value uplift by permit year, split between the actually-measured portion and the portion estimated with the model.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Cumulative estimated assessed value uplift since ZBR, by permit year (net of appreciation)](index_files/figure-html/fig-cumulative-value-uplift-1.png){#fig-cumulative-value-uplift width=672}
:::
:::


Adding it all up: since ZBR took effect, mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit rowhomes are estimated to have added **$1,373.48M** in gross assessed value, or **$1,321.47M** net of general market appreciation, across 118 (2024) + 302 (2025) + 399 (2026, projected) = 819 permitted projects.

@tbl-cumulative-selected-neighbourhoods breaks that cumulative estimate down for the same neighbourhoods used in the [Q2 2026 report's selected-neighbourhood table](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), places that have been prominent in debates about RS Zone infill. Each permit is valued at its actual measured uplift where available, or the model's prediction otherwise, then summed by neighbourhood. This covers every actual permit issued through H1 2026; the projected remainder of 2026 isn't tied to real permits yet, so it's included in the citywide total above but left out of this neighbourhood breakdown.


::: {#tbl-cumulative-selected-neighbourhoods .cell .column-page tbl-cap='Cumulative estimated uplift for selected neighbourhoods, 2024 through H1 2026'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

