## What this means for property tax

Assessed value only matters for the City's budget insofar as it's taxed. I convert the estimated uplift into two figures: an **annual recurring** estimate, at 2026 municipal tax rates, of how much more property tax this pipeline will generate every year once every project is complete and fully reflected in an assessment roll; and a rougher **cumulative collected so far** estimate that accounts for the fact that most of this pipeline is still under construction or only recently completed.

Completed 5-8 unit rowhomes on a single title are typically assessed as "Other Residential", which carries a higher municipal mill rate than the "Residential" class the original house was assessed under. In the measured cohort, 83% of completed properties land in Other Residential; I apply that same mix to the model-based portion of the estimate.


::: {.cell}

:::


At 2026 municipal tax rates, this pipeline is estimated to add **$10.75M** in property tax every year, net of what general appreciation would have generated anyway ($11.17M on a gross basis). That's before counting the provincial education levy, which would add roughly another third on top of the municipal portion.

Because most of this pipeline is still ramping up, actual tax collected so far is much lower than that steady-state figure. Comparing each matched property's mid-construction 2025 assessed value to its 2024 baseline and completed 2026 value shows how much of the total uplift typically shows up one year after a permit is issued.


::: {.cell}

:::


The median property has captured about 16% of its full uplift by the assessment one year after permitting, consistent with most projects still being under construction at that point. Modelling each year's cohort as contributing that partial share in the following year and the full amount from two years out, I estimate Edmonton has collected roughly **$2.41M** in incremental property tax from this pipeline across 2025 and 2026 combined, well short of the $10.75M-a-year run rate this pipeline will eventually reach once every project currently in the pipeline is complete and fully assessed.

These are estimates, not City budget figures. Assessment growth from new construction doesn't translate one-for-one into new City revenue, since Council sets the mill rate each year based on total budget needs, but new construction is genuinely additional tax base at whatever rate Council sets, distinct from a rate increase on existing properties. This also doesn't account for the cost of servicing new residents, which isn't the subject of this post.

## Conclusions

The RS Zone rowhome isn't just adding homes, it's rebuilding the property tax base of neighbourhoods that had been losing population and, with it, tax revenue per hectare of city-maintained infrastructure for decades. A handful of redevelopments per neighbourhood, concentrated in mature areas that already have the pipes, roads, and schools in place, is already worth tens of millions of dollars a year in additional property tax capacity, on top of the housing it delivers.

None of this required a subsidy, a grant, or a City-led development. It required removing rules that made this kind of building illegal. If you'd like to help keep it that way, consider joining [Grow Together Edmonton](https://www.growtogetheryeg.com/).
